 177	\section{Introduction}
 178	
 179	Quantum non-local games lie at the intersection of several areas of
 180	quantum information. They provide a natural approach to
 181	\emph{device-independent certification} or \emph{self-testing} of
 182	unknown quantum states. Device-independent
 183	certification has applications to quantum cryptography, from quantum
 184	key distribution~\cite{VV14,MS14} to delegated
 185	computation~\cite{ReichardtUV13nature,FitzsimonsH15}. The key idea
 186	behind these applications is that certain nonlocal games, such as the
 187	CHSH game~\cite{CHSH69}, provide natural statistical tests that can be
 188	used to certify that an arbitrary quantum device implements a certain
 189	``strategy'' specified by local measurements on an entangled state
 190	(e.g. an EPR pair).
 191	
 192	A common weakness of all existing self-testing results is that their
 193	performance scales poorly with the number of qubits of the state that
 194	is being tested. Given a self-test, define (somewhat informally) its
 195	robustness as the largest $\eps=\eps(\delta)$ such that a success
 196	probability at least $\omega^*_{\rm opt}-\eps$ in the test certifies the
 197	target state up to error (in trace distance and up to local isometries) at most $\delta$, where
 198	$\omega^*_{\rm opt}$ is the success probability achieved by an ideal
 199	strategy. All previously known tests for $n$-qubit states required
 200	$\eps \ll \poly(\delta,1/n)$.%, with an often high degree polynomial
 201	%dependence on both $\eps$ and $n$.
 202	
 203	Our main result is a form of robust self-test for {any} state that can
 204	be characterized via expectation values of tensor products of standard Pauli
 205	$\sigma_X$ or $\sigma_Z$ observables. (This includes a tensor product of $n$ EPR pairs; see below.)
 206	
 207	\begin{theorem}[simplified\footnote{The complete statement of
 208	the theorem says much more, and provides a characterization of near-optimal strategies.}]\label{thm:main_informal}
 209	  Let $\mathcal{P}$ be a set of $n$-qubit observables, each of
 210	  which is a tensor product of single-qubit Pauli $\sigma_X$, $\sigma_Z$ or
 211	  $\pm I$, and $\lambda_{\rm max} = \| \E_{P \sim \mathcal{P}} [\, P\,]\|$.  For any $\eta \geq 0$ there exists a
 212	  $p = p(\eta)=\Theta(\eta^c)$, where $0<c<1$ is a universal constant,
 213	  and a $7$-player nonlocal game with $O(n)$-bit questions and
 214	  $O(1)$-bit answers such that
 215		$$\omega^*_{\rm opt}\,
 216	 =\, \frac{1}{2} + p\, \lambda_{\rm max}\,\pm\, \eta.$$
 217	\end{theorem}
 218	
 219	We view the theorem as a robust self-test in the following
 220	sense. Suppose a many-qubit state $\ket{\psi}$ can be characterized as
 221	the leading eigenvector of an operator $O=\E_{P \sim \mathcal{P}} [P]$
 222	obtained as the average of $n$-qubit Pauli operators, with associated
 223	eigenvalue $\lambda_{\rm max}\in[-1,1]$. For example, if $\mathcal{P}$
 224	is the uniform distribution over
 225	$\{\sigma_X \otimes \sigma_X,\sigma_Z\otimes\sigma_Z\}^{\otimes n}$
 226	then $\lambda_{\rm \max}=1$ and the leading eigenvector is the tensor
 227	product of $n$ EPR pairs. More generally, if $H$ is a local
 228	Hamiltonian with $m$ local $XZ$ terms we can take $\mathcal{P}$ to be
 229	$\Id$ with probability $1/2$ and the negation of a random term of $H$
 230	with probability $1/2$. Then
 231	$\lambda_{\rm max} = \frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{2m}\lambda_{\rm min}(H)$ and
 232	the leading eigenvector is a ground state of $H$.
 233	
 234	Theorem~\ref{thm:main_informal} provides a nonlocal game such that the
 235	optimal success probability in the game is directly related to
 236	$\lambda_{\rm max}$, thereby providing a test distinguishing between
 237	small and large $\lambda_{\rm max}$. In fact the complete statement of
 238	the theorem (see Theorem~\ref{thm:main} in Section~\ref{sec:manyprovers}) says much
 239	more. In particular, we provide a complete characterization (up to
 240	local isometries) of strategies achieving a success probability at
 241	least $\omega^*_{\rm opt} - \eps$, for $\eps$ sufficiently small but independent of $n$, showing that such strategies must be based on a particular
 242	encoding (based on a simple, fixed error-correcting code) of an eigenvector associated to $\lambda_{\rm max}$. 
 243	
 244	\subsection{Applications}
 245	
 246	Before giving an overview of the proof of the theorem we discuss some consequences of the theorem that help underscore its generality.
 247	
 248	\paragraph{Hamiltonian complexity.} 
 249	A first consequence of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_informal} is that the ground state
 250	energy of a local Hamiltonian can be certified via a non-local game
 251	with questions of polynomial length and constant-length
 252	answers.%up to \emph{constant} error.
 253	
 254	\begin{corollary}\label{cor:qma}
 255	  Let $H$ be an $n$-qubit Hamiltonian that can be expressed as a weighted sum, with real coefficients, of tensor
 256	  products of $\sigma_X$ and $\sigma_Z$ operators on a subset of the
 257	  qubits, and normalize $H$ such that $\|H\|\leq 1$. Suppose it is given that $\lambda_{\rm min}(H) \leq a$ or
 258	  $\lambda_{\rm min}(H) \geq b$ for some $0\leq a<b\leq 1$. There exists a one-round interactive
 259	  proof protocol between a classical polynomial-time verifier and
 260	  $7$ entangled provers where the verifier's (classical)
 261	  questions are $O(n /(b-a))$ bits long, the provers' (classical)
 262	  answers are $O(1)$ bits each, and the maximum probability that the
 263	  verifier accepts is
 264	$$\lambda_{\rm min} \leq a \implies \omega^*_{\rm opt} \geq p_{\rm
 265	  c} := \frac{1}{2} +2\,\eta_0 ,\qquad \lambda_{\rm min} \geq
 266	b \implies \omega^*_{\rm opt} \leq p_{\rm s} := \frac{1}{2} + \eta_0,$$
 267	where $\eta_0>0$ is a small (universal) constant. 
 268	\end{corollary}
 269	
 270	Since the class of Hamiltonians considered in Corollary~\ref{cor:qma}
 271	is QMA-complete~\cite{CM13}, the corollary can be viewed as a
 272	quantum analogue of the (games variant of the) exponentially long PCP
 273	based on the linearity test of Blum, Luby and
 274	Rubinfeld~\cite{BLR93}.
 275	%\footnote{See~\cite[Section 5]{AharonovAV13qpcp} for a very different approach to the same question.} 
 276		Indeed, observe that the game constructed in the
 277	corollary has an efficient verifier, polynomial-length questions, and
 278	a constant completeness-soundness gap $\eta_0$ as soon as the original promise
 279	on the ground state energy for the Hamiltonian exhibits an
 280	inverse-polynomial completeness-soundness gap. The derivation of
 281	Corollary~\ref{cor:qma} from Theorem~\ref{thm:main_informal} involves a step of
 282	gap amplification via tensoring, and relies on the fact that
 283	Theorem~\ref{thm:main_informal} allows any $XZ$-Hamiltonian with no
 284	requirement on locality.
 285	
 286	A similar result to Corollary~\ref{cor:qma} was obtained by
 287	Ji~\cite{ji2015classical}, and we build on Ji's techniques. The results
 288	are incomparable: on the one hand, the question size in our protocol
 289	is much larger ($\poly(n)$ bits instead of $O(\log n)$
 290	for~\cite{ji2015classical}); on the other hand, the dependence of the
 291	verifier's acceptance probability on the ground state energy is much
 292	better, as in~\cite{ji2015classical} the completeness-soundness gap
 293	remains inverse polynomial.
 294	
 295	\paragraph{An exponential quantum PCP.} 
 296	The expert reader may already have noted that the complexity-theoretic
 297	formulation of Corollary~\ref{cor:qma} described above already follows
 298	from known results in quantum complexity. Indeed, recall that the
 299	class $\QMA$ is in $\PSPACE$, and that single-round multiprover
 300	interactive proof systems for $\PSPACE$ (and even $\NEXP$) follow from
 301	the results in~\cite{IV12,Vidick13xor}. Another possible proof
 302	approach for the same result could be obtained by repeating the
 303	protocol in~\cite{ji2015classical} a polynomial number of times;
 304	provided there existed an appropriate parallel repetition theorem this
 305	would amplify the soundness to a constant (although the answer length
 306	would now be polynomial). In fact, based on a recent result by Ji~\cite{ji16nexp} it seems likely that both approaches, based either on our results or parallel repetition of~\cite{ji16nexp}, could lead to an exponential ``quantum-games'' PCP for all languages in $\NEXP$ (instead of just $\QMA$). Even though in purely complexity-theoretic terms the result would still not be new, we believe that the techniques from Hamiltonian complexity developed to obtain it show good promise for further extensions. 
 307	
 308	Indeed our protocol has some advantages over the generic sequence of known reductions. One is efficiency: in our protocol the provers merely need
 309	access to a ground state of the given local Hamiltonian and the
 310	ability to perform constant-depth quantum circuits. It is this
 311	property that enables our application to delegated quantum computing
 312	(see below for more on this). Answers in our protocol are a constant number of bits; the reductions mentioned above would require soundness amplification via parallel repetition, which would lead to answers of (at least) linear length. 
 313	
 314	Even though they may not provide the most immediately compelling application of
 315	  Theorem~\ref{thm:main_informal}, the complexity-theoretic consequences of
 316	  Corollary~\ref{cor:qma} tie our results to one
 317	  of their primary motivations, the \emph{quantum PCP
 318	    conjecture}. Broadly speaking, the quantum PCP research program is
 319	  concerned with finding a robust analog of the Cook-Levin theorem for
 320	  the class $\QMA$. The ``games variant'' of this conjecture states
 321	  that estimating the optimal winning probability of entangled players
 322	  in a multiplayer nonlocal game, up to an additive constant, is
 323	  $\QMA$-hard. In other words, that there exists an $\MIP^*$ protocol
 324	  for $\QMA$ with $O(\log(n))$-bit messages and constant
 325	  completeness-soundness gap. The best progress to date in this
 326	  direction is the work of Ji~\cite{ji2015classical}, which gives a
 327	  five-prover one-round $\MIP^*$ protocol with $O(\log(n))$-bit
 328	  messages for the local Hamiltonian problem such that the verifier's
 329	  maximum acceptance probability is $a-b\lambda_{min}(H)n^{-c}$ for
 330	  positive constants $a,b,c$. This falls short of the games PCP
 331	  conjecture in that the completeness-soundness gap is inverse
 332	  polynomial in $n$, rather than constant.\footnote{Here again we point the interested reader to the recent~\cite{ji16nexp}, which obtains a protocol with similar parameters, involving $8$ provers, for all languages in $\NEXP$.}
 333	
