 378	\paragraph{Delegated computation.}
 379	It was noticed in~\cite{FitzsimonsH15} that an interactive proof
 380	system for the local Hamiltonian problem can also be used for
 381	delegated quantum computation with so-called \emph{post-hoc}
 382	verification. The key idea is to use the Feynman-Kitaev construction
 383	to produce a Hamiltonian encoding the desired computation; measuring
 384	the ground energy of this Hamiltonian reveals whether the computation
 385	accepts or rejects.
 386	%This connection was used in~\cite{FitzsimonsH15} to give a delegated computation scheme with a limited quantum verifier, with only the capability to send, receive, and measure a constant number of qubits. 
 387	Following the same connection, we are able to give a post-hoc
 388	verifiable delegated computation scheme with a purely classical
 389	verifier and a constant number of provers. The provers only need the
 390	power of BQP. The scheme has a constant completeness-soundness gap
 391	independent of the size of the circuit to be computed, unlike the
 392	scheme of~\cite{FitzsimonsH15} and the classical scheme
 393	of~\cite{ReichardtUV13nature}, which both have inverse-polynomial
 394	gaps. However, unlike the scheme of~\cite{ReichardtUV13nature}, our
 395	protocol is not \emph{blind}: the verifier must reveal the entire
 396	circuit to be computed to all the provers before the verification
 397	process starts. We refer to Section~\ref{sec:delegated} for more
 398	details on this application. 
