 408	\subsection{Proof overview}
 409	
 410	
 411	The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_informal} builds on ideas from complexity
 412	theory and quantum information. We draw inspiration from
 413	classical ideas in the closely related areas of probabilistically checakble proofs, locally
 414	testable codes, and property testing. The link between these
 415	areas and quantum self-testing is the idea of verifying a \emph{global}
 416	property of an unknown object using only limited measurements. The two most important components of
 417	the proof are a ``locally verifiable'' encoding of arbitrary $n$-qubit
 418	quantum states~\cite{FV14}, and a quantum analogue of the linearity
 419	test of Blum et al.~\cite{BLR93}. Since the second component is the
 420	more novel we explain it first.
 421	
 422	\paragraph{Linearity testing of quantum observables.}
 423	
 424	The simplest instantiation of the classical PCP theorem relies on the
 425	Hadamard code to robustly encode an $n$-bit string (e.g. an assignment
 426	to an instance of $3$-SAT). Under this code,
 427	a string $u \in \{0,1\}^n$ is encoded as the $2^n$-bit long truth table of the
 428	function $f_u:\{0,1\}^n \to \{-1, 1\}$ given by $f_u(x) = (-1)^{u
 429	  \cdot x}$, where $\cdot$ is the bitwise inner product. The function
 430	$f_u(x)$ is said to be \emph{linear}, since $f_u(x+y) = f_u(x)f_u(y)$.
 431	The key property of the Hadamard code
 432	which makes it useful in this context is that it is locally
 433	testable. A local test is given by the BLR linearity test: given query
 434	access to a function $f:\{0,1\}^n\to\{-1,1\}$, by checking that
 435	$f(x+y)=f(x)f(y)$ at randomly chosen $x,y$ the test certifies that any
 436	$f$ that is accepted with probability at least $1-\eps$ has the form
 437	$f\approx_\eps f_u$ for some $u\in\{0,1\}^n$, where
 438	$f_u:a\mapsto (-1)^{u\cdot a}$ and $\approx_\eps$ designates equality
 439	on an $(1-O(\eps))$ fraction of inputs.
 440	
 441	Here is a ``quantum'' reformulation of this test as a nonlocal game:
 442	instead of querying an oracle for $f$ at three points, play a
 443	three-player nonlocal game where each player is asked for the value
 444	at a point. This test is sound even if the players share an entangled quantum
 445	state~\cite{IV12}, but success in the test does not certify quantum behavior:
 446	the players could win with certainty just by sharing a description of
 447	a classical linear function $f_u$; indeed, the main point of the analysis in~\cite{IV12} is precisely to ensure that provers sharing entanglement have no more freedom than to use it as shared randomness in selecting $u$. 
 448	
 449	 In contrast, we seek an extension of the test which certifies a very specific type of quantum behavior that could not be emulated by classical means alone: specifically, that the observable $O_x$ measured by a player upon receiving question $x$ itself
 450	is (up to a change of basis, and in the appropriate ``state-dependent'' norm) close to $\otimes_i \sigma_X^{x_i}$.
 451	%---any
 452	%successful strategy should be equivalent under an isometry to
 453	%measuring all qubits of an $n$-qubit state to obtain an $n$-bit string
 454	%$u$, and then returning the value of $f_u$ at the queried point $x$.
 455	We give a test which achieves this. The test performs a combination of
 456	a linearity test in the $X$-basis and a linearity test in the
 457	$Z$-basis; an ``anticommutation game'' (which can be taken to be a
 458	version of the CHSH or Magic Square games) is used to constrain how the
 459	results of the two linearity tests relate to each other.
 460	
 461	
 462	\begin{theorem}[Pauli braiding test, informal]\label{thm:braiding}
 463	  There exists a two-player nonlocal game, based on the combination of
 464	  (i) a linearity test in the $X$ basis (questions $x\in\{0,1\}^n$);
 465	  (ii) a linearity test in the $Z$ basis (questions $z\in\{0,1\}^n$;
 466	  (iii) an ``anticommutation game'' (based on e.g. the CHSH or Magic Square games) designed to test for generalized   anti-commutation relations (questions $(x,z)\in\{0,1\}^{2n}$), such
 467	  that any strategy
 468	%$\ket{\psi}_{AB}$, $\{P^A_{x,z}\}$, $\{P^B_{x,z}\}$ 
 469	  that has success probability $\omega^*_{\rm opt}-\eps$ for some
 470	  $\eps>0$ must be based on observables $A(x),A(z),A(x,z)$ and an
 471	  entangled state $\ket{\psi}_{AB}$ such that up to local isometries
 472	  $$A(x) \approx_{\delta} \otimes_i \sigma_X^{x_i}, \qquad A(z) \approx_{\delta} \otimes_i \sigma_Z^{z_i},\qquad\text{and}
 473	\qquad \ket{\psi}_{AB} \approx_{\delta} \ket{\epr}_{AB}^{\otimes n},$$
 474	where $\delta=\poly(\eps)$. 
 475	\end{theorem}
 476	
 477	Neither the linearity test nor the
 478	anticommutation test \emph{alone} would be  sufficient to achieve the
 479	conclusion: as noted above, the linearity test can be passed even by
 480	classical provers, and our anticommutation test can be fooled if the
 481	provers share just one EPR pair. Rather, it is the guarantees provided
 482	by these tests together that enable us to create a tensor-product
 483	structure in the provers' Hilbert space. 
 484	
 485	To gain intuition on the test one may think of it in the following way. A standard approach to self-testing $n$ EPR pairs is to fix a decomposition of the Hilbert space as 
 486	\begin{equation}\label{eq:c2n}
 487	\mH = \C^{2^n} \approx \C^2 \otimes \cdots \otimes \C^2,
 488	\end{equation}
 489	and perform the CHSH (or Magic Square) test ``in parallel'', on each copy of $\C^2$. To the best of our knowledge such test only leads to robustness bounds with a polynomial dependence in $n$. In contrast the test on which Theorem~\ref{thm:braiding} is based relies on the observation that the decomposition~\eqref{eq:c2n} need not be rigidly fixed a priori; indeed there are many bases in which such decomposition of $\C^{2^n}$ in tensor factors can be performed. In particular,  \emph{any} pair of anti-commuting observables on $\mH$ suffices to specify a copy of $\C^2$, on which a CHSH test can in principle be performed (here we crucially rely on rotation invariance of the $2^n$-dimensional maximally entangled state). Our test leverages this observation by performing a CHSH test for each possible pair of Pauli operators ($\sigma_X(a)$, $\sigma_Z(b)$), where $a,b\in\{0,1\}^n$ are such that $a\cdot b=1$. Each of these tests amounts to identifying a copy of $\C^2$ and performing the CHSH test on it. Contrary to the parallel-repeated CHSH test these copies are not independent, and this is what makes our test much more robust. 
 490	
 491	\paragraph{Encoding quantum states.}
 492	The second component of the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_informal} is a
 493	procedure, first introduced in~\cite{FV14,ji2015classical}, for
 494	encoding an $n$-qubit quantum state in a constant number of $n$-qubit
 495	shares such that certain properties of the encoded state (such as
 496	expectation values of local Pauli observables) can be verified through
 497	a classical interaction with provers each holding one of the
 498	shares. This is akin to how the ``games'' variant of the classical PCP
 499	theorem is derived from the ``proof-checking'' variant: while in the
 500	classical setting a proof can be directly shared across multiple
 501	provers, in the quantum setting we use a form of secret-sharing code
 502	that allows for distributing quantum information.
 503	% Given a state of interest (such as the ground state of a local
 504	% Hamiltonian), the expectation value of a local $XZ$ Pauli operator
 505	% on the state can be estimated by a combination of tests involving a
 506	% stabilizer-state generalization of the CHSH state~\cite{Ji}, which
 507	% depends on the precise encoding used.
 508	
 509	This procedure is efficient in that the total number of tests that can
 510	be performed (equivalently, the number of questions) is polynomial in
 511	$n$. However, the test in~\cite{FV14,ji2015classical} is not robust,
 512	and is only able to provide meaningful results for values of $\eps$
 513	that scale inverse-polynomially with $n$. By extending the Pauli
 514	braiding test, Theorem~\ref{thm:braiding}, to the stabilizer framework
 515	of~\cite{ji2015classical} we obtain a procedure which is meaningful
 516	for constant $\eps$. The drawback is that the provers may now be asked
 517	to measure all their qubits, and questions have length linear in $n$;
 518	however the total effort required of the classical verifier (and of provers given access to the state) remains
 519	polynomial in the size of the instance.
