 705	\section{Preliminaries}
 706	\label{sec:prelim}
 707	%----------------------%
 708	
 709	We assume basic familiarity with quantum information but give all required definitions. We refer to the standard textbook~\cite{NieChu01} for additional background material. 
 710	
 711	\subsection{Quantum states and measurements}
 712	
 713	A $n$-qubit pure quantum state is represented by a unit vector
 714	$\ket{\psi}\in \C^2 \otimes \cdots \otimes \C^2 = (\C^2)^{\otimes n}
 715	\approx \C^{2^n}$,
 716	where the ket notation $\ket{\cdot}$ is used to signify a column
 717	vector. A bra $\bra{\psi}$ is used for the conjugate-transpose
 718	$\bra{\psi} = \ket{\psi}^\dagger$, which is a row vector. We use
 719	$\|\ket{\psi}\|^2 = |\bra{\psi}\psi\rangle|$ to denote the Euclidean
 720	norm, where $\bra{\psi}\phi\rangle$ is the skew-Hermitian inner
 721	product between vectors $\ket{\phi}$ and $\ket{\psi}$. A $n$-qubit
 722	mixed state is represented by a density matrix, a positive
 723	semi-definite matrix $\rho \in \C^{2^n} \times \C^{2^n}$ of trace
 724	$1$. The density matrix associated to $\ket{\psi}$ is the rank-1
 725	projection $\ket{\psi}\bra{\psi}$. We use $\Density(\mH)$ to denote the set of all density matrices on $\mH$. 
 726	
 727	For a matrix $X$, $\|X\|$ will refer to the operator norm, the largest
 728	singular value. When the Hilbert space can be decomposed as
 729	$\h = \h_A \otimes \h_B$ for some $\h_A$ and $\h_B$, and $X$ is an
 730	operator on $\h_A$, we often write $X$ as well for the operator
 731	$X\otimes\Id_{\h_B}$ on $\h$. It will always be clear from context
 732	which space an operator acts on. All Hilbert spaces considered in the paper are finite dimensional. 
 733	
 734	We use $\Pos(\mH)$ to denote the set of positive semidefinite operators on $\mH$. 
 735	A $n$-qubit measurement (also called POVM, for projective
 736	operator-valued measurement) with $k$ outcomes is specified by $k$
 737	positive matrices $M=\{M_1,\ldots,M_k\} \subseteq \Pos(\C^{2^n})$
 738	such that $\sum_i M_i = \Id$. The measurement is \emph{projective} if
 739	each $M_i$ is a projector, i.e. $M_i^2 = M_i$. The probability of
 740	obtaining the $i$-th outcome when measuring state $\rho$ with $M$ is
 741	$\Tr(M_i \rho)$. By Naimark's dilation theorem, any POVM can be
 742	simulated by a projective measurement acting on an enlarged state;
 743	that is, for every POVM $M = \{M_i\}_i$ acting on state
 744	$\ket{\psi} \in \mathcal{H}$ there exists a projective measurement
 745	$M' = \{P_i\}_i$ and a state
 746	$\ket{\psi}\otimes \ket{\phi} \in \mathcal{H} \otimes
 747	\mathcal{H}_{\text{ancilla}}$
 748	with the same outcome probabilities as $M$. Moreover, the
 749	post-measurement state after performing $M$ is the same as the
 750	\emph{reduced} post-measurement state obtained after performing $M'$
 751	and tracing out the ancilla subsystem $\mathcal{H}_{\text{ancilla}}$.
 752	
 753	An $n$-qubit observable is a Hermitian matrix
 754	$O\in \C^{2^n}\times\C^{2^n}$ that squares to identity. We use $\Obs(\mH)$ to denote the set of observables acting on $\mH$. $O\in\Obs(\mH)$ is
 755	diagonalizable with eigenvalues $\pm 1$, $O=P_+-P_-$, and
 756	$P=\{P_+,P_-\}$ is a projective measurement. For any state $\rho$,
 757	$\Tr(O\rho)$ is the expectation of the $\pm 1$ outcome obtained when
 758	measuring $\rho$ with $P$. If $\rho = \ket{\psi}\bra{\psi}$ we
 759	abbreviate this quantity,
 760	$\Tr(O\rho) = \Tr(P_+\rho)-\Tr(P_-\rho) = \langle \psi | O | \psi
 761	\rangle$ as $\avg{P}{\psi}$.
 762	
 763	A convenient orthogonal basis for the real vector space of $n$-qubit observables is given by the set $\{I,\sigma_X,\sigma_Y,\sigma_Z\}^{\otimes n}$, where $\{I,\sigma_X,\sigma_Y,\sigma_Z\}$ are the four single-qubit Pauli observables
 764	\begin{equation}\label{eq:pauli-def}
 765	 I = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix},\quad
 766	\sigma_X= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \sigma_Y = \begin{pmatrix} 0
 767	  & -i \\ i & 0\end{pmatrix}, \quad \sigma_Z = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 &
 768	  -1\end{pmatrix} .
 769		\end{equation}
 770	We call the eigenbasis of $\sigma_X$ (resp. $\sigma_Z$) the $X$-basis (resp. $Z$-basis). 
 771	We 
 772	often consider operators that are tensor products of just
 773	$I$ and $\sigma_X$, or just $I$ and $\sigma_Z$. We denote these by $\sigma_X(a), \sigma_Z(b)$, where
 774	the strings $a, b \in \{0, 1\}^n$ indicate which qubits to apply the
 775	$\sigma_X$ or $\sigma_Z$ operators to: a $0$ in position $i$ indicates an $I$ on
 776	qubit $i$, and a $1$ indicates an $\sigma_X$ or $\sigma_Z$. 
 777	We denote by 
 778	$$\ket{\EPR} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \ket{0}\ket{0} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\ket{1}\ket{1}$$
 779	the unique state stabilized by both $\sigma_X\otimes\sigma_X$ and
 780	$\sigma_Z\otimes \sigma_Z$. 
 781	
 782	
 783	\subsection{Stabilizer codes}
 784	\label{sec:stabilizer}
 785	
 786	Stabilizer codes are the quantum analogue of linear codes. For an introduction to the theory of stabilizer codes we refer to~\cite{Gottesman97}. We will only use very elementary properties of such codes. 
 787	
 788	The codes we consider are \emph{Calderbank-Shor-Steane (CSS)
 789	  codes}~\cite{CalderbankShor96,Steane96}. For an $r$-qubit code the
 790	codespace, the vector space of all valid codewords, is the subspace of
 791	$(\C^2)^{\otimes r}$ that is the simultaneous $+1$ eigenspace of a set
 792	$\{S_1,\ldots,S_k\}$ of $r$-qubit pairwise commuting Pauli observables
 793	called the stabilizers of the code. The stabilizers form a group under
 794	multiplication. Unitary operations, such as a Pauli $X$ or $Z$
 795	operators, on the logical qubit are implemented on the codespace by
 796	logical operators $X_{logical}$ and $Z_{logical}$. The smallest CSS code is Steane's $7$-qubit code~\cite{Steane96}. Table~\ref{tab:stabilizer} lists a set of stabilizers that generate the stabilizer group of the code. 
 797	
 798	\begin{table}
 799	  \centering
 800	  \begin{tabular}[h]{l | C C C C C C R }
 801	    &1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 \\ \hline
 802	    Stabilizers &I & I & I & \sigma_X & \sigma_X  & \sigma_X & \sigma_X \\
 803	    &I & \sigma_X & \sigma_X & I & I & \sigma_X & \sigma_X \\
 804	    &\sigma_X & I & \sigma_X & I & \sigma_X & I & \sigma_X \\
 805	    &I & I & I & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z  & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z \\
 806	    &I & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z & I & I & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z \\
 807	    &\sigma_Z & I & \sigma_Z & I & \sigma_Z & I & \sigma_Z \\ \hline
 808	    Logical X & \sigma_X & \sigma_X & \sigma_X & \sigma_X & \sigma_X & \sigma_X & \sigma_X \\
 809	    Logical Z & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z & \sigma_Z
 810	  \end{tabular}
 811	  \caption{Stabilizer table for the 7-qubit Steane code}
 812	  \label{tab:stabilizer}
 813	\end{table}
 814			
 815	Every CSS code satisfies certain properties which will be useful for
 816	us. Firstly, both the stabilizer generators and the logical operators
 817	can be written as tensor products of only $I$, $\sigma_X$, and $\sigma_Z$ operators --- there are no
 818	$\sigma_Y$. This simplifies our protocol, allowing us to consider only two
 819	distinct basis settings. Secondly, every CSS code has the following symmetry: for every index $i \in [r]$ there
 820	exists stabilizers $S_X$, $S_Z$ such that $S_X$ is a tensor product of
 821	only $\sigma_X$ and $I$ operators and has an $\sigma_X$ at position
 822	$i$, and $S_Z$ is equal to $S_X$ with all $\sigma_X$ operators replaced by
 823	$\sigma_Z$ operators. 
 824	
 825	These properties imply the following simple observation,
 826	which will be important for us. For every Pauli operator $P\in\{I,\sigma_X,\sigma_Z\}$ acting on the $i$-th qubit of the code there is a tensor product $\bar{P}$ of Paulis acting on the remaining $(r - 1)$ qubits such that $P \otimes \bar{P}$ is a stabilizer
 827	operator on the whole state, and moreover each term in the tensor
 828	product is either identity or $P$. Indeed, the choice of $\bar{P}$ is not unique. Henceforth, we use the
 829	notion $\bar{P}$ to denote \emph{any} such operator, unless
 830	otherwise specified. 
 831	
 832	\subsection{Local Hamiltonians}\label{sec:local-h}
 833	
 834	A $n$-qubit local Hamiltonian is a Hermitian, positive semidefinite  operator $H$ on $(\C^2)^{\otimes {n}}$ that can be decomposed as a sum $H = \sum_{i=1}^m H_i$ with each $H_i$ is local, i.e. $H_i$ can be written as $H_i = I\otimes\cdots I \otimes h_i \otimes I \otimes \cdots \otimes I$, where $h_i$ is a Hermitian operator on $(\C^2)^{\otimes k}$ with norm (largest singular value) at most $1$. The smallest $k$ for which $H$ admits such a decomposition is called the locality of $H$. The terms are normalized such that $\| H_i\| \leq 1$ for all $i$. A family of Hamiltonians $\{H_i\}$ acting on increasing numbers of qubits is called local if all $H_i$ are $k$-local for some $k$ independent of $n$ (for us $k$ will always be $2$).
 835	
 836	The local Hamiltonian problem is the prototypical $\QMA$-complete problem, as 3SAT is for $\NP$. 
 837	
 838	\begin{definition}
 839	Let $k\geq 2$ be an integer. 
 840	  The $k$-\emph{local Hamiltonian problem} is to decide, given a
 841	  family of $k$-local Hamiltonians $\{H_n\}_{n\in\N}$ such that $H_n$ acts on $n$ qubits, and functions $a,b:\N\to (0,1)$ such that $b - a = \Omega( \poly^{-1}(n))$, if the smallest eigenvalue
 842	  of $H_n$ is less than $a(n)$ or greater than $b(n)$.
 843	\end{definition}
 844	
 845	Here we restrict our attention to Hamiltonians 
 846	\[ H = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} H_i, \]
 847	for which each term $H_i$ can be written as a linear combination of
 848	tensor products of Pauli $I$, $\sigma_X$ and $\sigma_Z$ observables only. Such
 849	Hamiltonians are known to be QMA complete for some constant $k$ (see Lemma~22
 850	of~\cite{ji2015classical} for a proof).
 851	% ; in particular we consider a
 852	% restricted class of $2$-local Hamiltonians, called Hamiltonians of
 853	% $XZ$ form, for which each $H_i$ can be
 854	% written as
 855	% $H_i = \alpha_{i_1 i_2}(X_{i_1} \otimes X_{i_2} + Z_{i_1} \otimes
 856	% Z_{i_2})$,
 857	% where $i_1,i_2\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$ indicate the qubits on which a Pauli
 858	% $X$ or $Z$ acts and the coefficients $\alpha_{i_1i_2}\in\R$ satisfy
 859	% $|\alpha_{i_1i_2}|\leq 1$.
 860	
