1602	\section{The Hamiltonian Self-Test}
1603	\label{sec:manyprovers}
1604	%----------------------%
1605	
1606	In this section, we build on the Pauli braiding test to construct a test that distinguishes between the cases when a Hamiltonian given as input has ground state energy below, or higher than, pre-specified thresholds (i.e. in the former case the players will have a strategy with high success probability in the protocol, whereas in the latter any strategy will have low success probability).
1607	Due to the nature of our tests we restrict attention to $n$-qubit Hamiltonians specified by a linear combination of $m$ terms, each of which is a tensor product of single-qubit $I, \sigma_X$ or $\sigma_Z$ Pauli operators. 
1608	
1609	Recall the Pauli braiding test analyzed in the previous section. As we saw (Corollary~\ref{cor:epr-test}) this test can be used as a robust self-test for an $n$-qubit maximally entangled state. In order to test non-maximally entangled states, we
1610	proceed as in~\cite{FV14,ji2015classical} by requiring the (honest) players to share a qubit-by-qubit encoding of the ground state of the Hamiltonian, where each qubit is encoded using a simple $r$-qubit CSS code. As elucidated
1611	in~\cite{ji2015classical}, any code state, thought of as a bipartite entangled state
1612	across any one of its qubits and the others, is maximally entangled. This allows us to lift the two-player tests which constitute the Pauli braiding test to $r$-player tests, where each player holds one qubit (``share'') of the encoding of each qubit of the ground state, and one of
1613	the players (to be called the \emph{special player}) plays the role of Alice while the remaining $(r - 1)$ players (to be called the \emph{composite player})
1614	play the role of Bob. 
1615	
1616	The essential property of the consituent tests of the Pauli braiding test that permit this lifting is that all of the measurements performed by Bob in the honest
1617	strategy can be implemented by measuring the tensor
1618	product of Pauli operators $\sigma_X$, $\sigma_Z$, and $\Id$ on a state of $n$
1619	EPR pairs. (For the anticommutation test, this is ensured by the completeness
1620	condition in Definition~\ref{def:ac-game}, and for the other tests, it can be
1621	seen to hold for both Alice and Bob's measurements). These operators can be implemented
1622	transversally in any CSS code, and moreover in a way such that marginal
1623	distribution of queries received by the special player and each of the composite
1624	players is identical, as we show below. 
1625	
1626	
1627	\subsection{The protocol} 
1628	
1629	We describe the protocol in detail. The input is an $n$-qubit
1630	local Hamiltonian $H$ that can be expressed as
1631	\begin{equation}\label{eq:h-form}
1632	H = \frac{1}{m}\sum_{\ell=1}^m H_\ell, \qquad H_\ell = \alpha_\ell \,\sigma_X(a_\ell)\sigma_Z(b_\ell),
1633	\end{equation}
1634	for $\alpha_\ell \in [-1,1]$ and  $a_\ell,b_\ell\in\{0,1\}^n$ such that $a_\ell\wedge b_\ell=0^n$ for all $\ell\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$. 
1635	 The verifier interacts with $r$
1636	players, where $r$ is the number of qubits of codewords in the CSS code chosen for the protocol (such as Steane's $7$-qubit code, as described in Section~\ref{sec:stabilizer},  in which case $r=7$).
1637	
1638	
1639	\begin{figure}[H]
1640	\rule[1ex]{16.5cm}{0.5pt}\\
1641	Given a local Hamiltonian $H = \sum_{\ell=1}^m \alpha_\ell H_\ell$, where $\alpha_\ell \in [-1,1]$ and each $H_\ell = \sigma_X(a_\ell)\sigma_{Z}(b_\ell)$. Let $p\in(0,1)$ be a parameter of the protocol.
1642	
1643	The verifier performs one of the following three tests at random, the first with probability $(1-p)$ and the second and third with probability $p/2$ each. 
1644	\begin{enumerate}
1645	\item (Pauli braiding test) Choose one of the $r$ players uniformly at random to be the
1646	  {special player}. The other players form the {composite
1647	     player}. Simulate the Pauli braiding test with these two
1648	   players, where the role of Alice is assigned to the special player and the role of Bob to the composite player.  
1649	\item (Energy test) Choose $\ell\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$ uniformly at random. Define an operator $Q_\ell$ acting on $rn$ qubits by replacing each $\sigma_X$ in $H_\ell$ with $X_{logical}$ on the $r$-qubit code
1650	  state, and $\sigma_Z$ by $Z_{logical}$. Send each player a query $(XZ,a,b)$ representing the associated share of $Q_\ell$. The players should each return two values in $\{-1,1\}$.\\
1651		The verifier takes the product of all values received. If its sign disagrees with that of $\alpha_\ell$, he accepts. If they agree, he rejects with probability $|\alpha_\ell|$ and accepts otherwise. 
1652	\item (Energy consistency test) Choose one of the $r$ players uniformly at random to be the
1653	  {special player}. The other players form the {composite
1654	     player}. Let $W\in\{X,Z\}$, each chosen with probability
1655	   $1/2$. Also choose $a, b$ according to the same distribution as in the
1656	   energy test. The verifier performs one of the following tests, each
1657	   chosen with the indicated probability.
1658	\begin{itemize}
1659	\item With probability $1/2$, send the special player $(XZ,a,b)$, and the composite player $\overline{(W, c, c + a)}$ if $W=X$ and $\overline{(W, c, c + b)}$ if $W=Z$, where $c \in \{0, 1\}^n$ is chosen uniformly at random. Accept if the special player's answer agrees with the product of the composite player's two answers.
1660	\item With probability $1/4$, send the special player $(W, c, d)$, and the composite player $\overline{(W, c, c + a)}$, where $c, d \in \{0, 1\}^n$ are chosen uniformly at random. Accept if the special player and composite player agree on the answer associated with $c$.
1661	\item With probability $1/4$, send the special player $(W, c+a,d)$, and the composite player $\overline{(W, c, c + a)}$, where $c, d \in \{0, 1\}^n$ are chosen uniformly at random. Accept if the special player and composite player agree on the answer associated with $c+a$.
1662	\end{itemize}
1663	\end{enumerate}
1664	\rule[1ex]{16.5cm}{0.5pt}\\
1665	\caption{The Hamiltonian self-test}
1666	\label{fig:protocol}
1667	\end{figure}
1668	
1669	Although the protocol is to be performed with $r$ ``physical''
1670	players, part of the protocol consists in applying the Pauli braiding test, which is formulated as a two-player test in the previous section. To translate between the $r$ players and the two players in the Pauli braiding test we introduce two ``logical'' players. A query to the logical players (as specified in the Pauli braiding test) is mapped to a query to the $r$ physical players as follows. One of the physical players is chosen at random to play the role of the first logical player (Alice), called the
1671	\emph{special player}. The remaining $(r-1)$ physical players together play the role of the second logical player (Bob), called the \emph{composite player}.\footnote{The physical players remain isolated throughout the protocol and are never allowed to communicate; it is only for purposes of analysis that we group $(r-1)$ physical players into a single logical player. In particular the physical players are never told which logical player they are associated with, and the distribution of queries to any physical player is the same whether it plays the role of the special or composite player.} For a given query $Q$ to the special player of a type among those specified in the Pauli braiding test we define a \emph{complementary query} $\overline{Q}$ for the composite player as per the following lemma.     
1672	
1673	\begin{lemma}
1674	  For any $X$-query or $Z$-query, there exists a complementary query $\overline{Q}$ such that
1675	  \begin{enumerate}
1676	  \item The query associated to each physical player forming the composite player in $\overline{Q}$ is of the same type as $Q$. In particular the distribution on query strings is as specified by the query type. 
1677	  \item If all players apply the honest strategy and provide answers $\alpha,\beta$ to $Q$ and  $\overline{\alpha},\overline{\beta}$ to $\overline{Q}$ respectively, where $\overline{\alpha}$ and $\overline{\beta}$ are each obtained as the product of the answer to the corresponding query coming from each of the physical players making up the composite player, it holds that $\alpha\overline{\alpha} = \beta\overline{\beta}=+1$.
1678	  \end{enumerate}
1679	  \label{lem:qbar}
1680	\end{lemma}
1681	
1682	\begin{proof}
1683	  Both items follow from the properties of CSS codes described in Section~\ref{sec:stabilizer}. We give the proof for an
1684	  $X$-query $(X, a, b)$. Let the index
1685	  of the special player be $i\in\{1,\ldots,r\}$, and let $S_X$ be a stabilizer
1686	  of the code, such that $S_X$ consists only of $I$ and $\sigma_X$ Paulis and has a $\sigma_X$ in
1687	  position $i$. For each physical
1688	  player $j \neq i$ associated with the composite player, if the operator in
1689	  position $j$ of $S_X$ is $\sigma_X$,  player $j$ is sent the query $(X,
1690	  a, b)$. Otherwise, player $j$ is sent a uniformly random $X$-query
1691	  $(X, c, d)$.
1692		
1693			Composite answers $\overline{\alpha},\overline{\beta}$ to the complementary
1694	    query are determined by taking the product of the answers from all
1695	    players who did not receive random strings; using that $S_X$ is a stabilizer of the code  ensures that item 2 is satisfied. 
1696		
1697		In the composite query, for a given choice of $S_X$ each player
1698	  receives a query that is either identical to the original query, or
1699	  is a uniformly random string; since the original query is chosen at random this is also the case for each of the physical players associated with the composite player. This proves item 1.
1700	\end{proof}
1701	
1702	We can then define associated observables for the players, $\hat{X}(a)$ and $\hat{Z}(b)$ for the special player and $\overline{X}(a)$ and $\overline{Z}(b)$ for the composite player, exactly as in~\eqref{eq:hatx-def}. 
1703	
1704	\begin{definition}\label{def:ol-obs}
1705	Let $\{\hat{M}_{a,b}^{\alpha\beta}\}$ (resp. $\{\overline{M}_{a,b}^{\alpha\beta}\}$) be the POVM implemented by the special player (resp. composite player) when asked a query $(W,a,b)$ (resp $(\overline{W},a,b)$; see Lemma~\ref{lem:qbar}), for $W=X$ or $Z$. Define observables
1706	\begin{equation*}
1707	\hat{W}(a) = \frac{1}{2^n}\sum_{b\in\{0,1\}^n} \sum_{\beta\in \{\pm1\}} \big(\hat{M}_{a,b}^{1\beta}-\hat{M}_{a,b}^{-1\beta}\big), \qquad \overline{W}(a) = \frac{1}{2^n}\sum_{b\in\{0,1\}^n} \sum_{\beta\in \{\pm1\}} \big(\overline{M}_{a,b}^{1\beta}-\overline{M}_{a,b}^{-1\beta}\big).
1708	\end{equation*}
1709	\end{definition}
1710	
1711	Aside from the Pauli braiding test, the protocol considers two other
1712	tests called the \emph{energy test} and the \emph{energy consistency
1713	  test}. In the energy test, the verifier asks the players to measure
1714	a randomly chosen term in the Hamiltonian. The consistency test is
1715	needed to relate the operators applied in the energy test
1716	to those applied in the Pauli braiding test.
1717	The energy test uses an additional query type, which differs from the types of queries used in the Pauli braiding test:
1718	\begin{enumerate}
1719	\item[3.] An \emph{$XZ$-query} is represented by $(XZ, a, b)$ where $a,b\in\{0,1\}^n$ are  such that $a\wedge b = 0^n$. Note that here, in contrast to $X-$ or $Z-$queries, the strings $a$ and $b$ are ordered. The distribution on $a$ and $b$ depends on the Hamiltonian. The expected answer is two bits $\alpha,\beta\in\{-1,1\}$.
1720	\end{enumerate}
1721	
1722	The honest strategy for the players in the Hamiltonian self-test (Figure~\ref{fig:protocol}) consists of applying the honest strategy defined for the Pauli braiding test (Definition~\ref{def:honest-strategy}) whenever the query is of $X$, $Z$, or $G$ type, and the following strategy when it is of $XZ$ type:
1723	
1724	\begin{definition}\label{def:honest-h}
1725	In the honest strategy, a player answers an $XZ$-query $(XZ,a,b)$ by measuring the compatible observables $\sigma_X(a)$ and $\sigma_Z(b)$ and returning both outcomes.
1726	\end{definition}
1727	
1728	
1729	
1730	\subsection{Statement of results}
1731	
1732	Our main result regarding the Hamiltonian self-test is given in the following theorem, which states the completeness and soundness guarantees of the protocol described in Figure~\ref{fig:protocol}.
1733	
1734	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
1735	There exists a constant $0<d<1$ such that the following holds. 
1736	  Let $H$ be a (not necessarily local) Hamiltonian with $m$ terms over $n$ qubits of the form~\eqref{eq:h-form}, and
1737	  $\lambda_{\min}(H)$ the smallest eigenvalue of $H$. Then for every $\eta>0$ there is a choice $p=\Theta(\eta^{1-d})$ for the probability of performing the energy test  in Protocol~\ref{fig:protocol} such that the maximum probability $\omega^*(H)$ with which any $r$-player strategy succeeds in the protocol satisfies
1738	  \[ 1 - \frac{p}{8}\Big(\lambda_{min}(H) + \frac{2}{m}\sum_{\ell=1}^m |\alpha_l|\Big) \,\leq\, \omega^*(H) \,\leq\, 1 - \frac{p}{8}\Big(\lambda_{min}(H) + \frac{2}{m}\sum_{\ell=1}^m |\alpha_l|\Big) + \eta. \]
1739	\end{theorem}
1740	
1741	Corollary~\ref{cor:qma} follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:main} by an amplification step described in Section~\ref{sec:amplification}. The proof of the theorem relies on the analysis of the energy test and the energy consistency test, given in Section~\ref{sec:energytest} and Section~\ref{sec:consistencytest} respectively, together with the analysis of the Pauli braiding test given in Section~\ref{sec:twoplayer}. 
1742	
1743	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.]
1744	  First we establish the lower bound. 
1745	  An honest quantum strategy (as described in Definition~\ref{def:honest-strategy} and Definition~\ref{def:honest-h}) acting on an encoded ground state $\ket{\Gamma}$ of $H$, together with an encoding of the additional EPR pairs $\ket{\EPR}^{\otimes (m-1)}_{A'B'}$ required to implement an optimal strategy in the anticommutation game $G_\ac$ (recall we take $G_\ac=\MS$ in this section, so $\wg=1$, $\delta(\eps)=O(\sqrt{\eps})$, and $m=2$) succeeds in the protocol with probability
1746	$ \omega_{\rm honest}(H) = (1-p) + p\, \omega^*_{\energy}(H)$,
1747	where
1748	\begin{align*}
1749	 \omega^*_{\energy}(H) &=\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2}\Big( 1 -
1750	    \frac{1}{4} \lambda_{\min}(H)  - \frac{1}{2m} \sum_{\ell=1}^m |\alpha_{\ell}| \Big)
1751	\end{align*}
1752	denotes the probability of the honest strategy to pass in the energy and consistency tests, each executed with probability $1/2$; the analysis of the energy test is from Lemma~\ref{lem:energy_test}.
1753	
1754	Next we establish the upper bound. Suppose a strategy for the players succeeds 
1755	  with overall probability $\omega_{\rm cheat}$, passes the Pauli braiding test with probability $1 -
1756	  \eps$, and passes the energy and consistency tests with probability
1757	  $\omega_{\energy}$; thus $\omega_{\rm cheat} = (1-p)(1 - \eps) + p \,\omega_{\energy}$.
1758		Applying the combination of Theorem~\ref{thm:gametwoplayer} and Theorem~\ref{thm:isotwoplayer} there exists an $(rn)$-qubit state $\ket{\varphi_1}$ on which the action of the Pauli operators $\sigma_X, \sigma_Z$ is $O(\eps^{1/8})$-consistent with the action of the players' operators $X, Z$ in the cheating strategy. Further, \lemref{energy_consistency} shows that the measurements performed in the energy test are $O(\eps^{d})$-consistent, for some $0<d<1$, with the corresponding product of players' $X$ and $Z$ operators from the Pauli test. Combining these two statements we deduce that an \emph{honest} strategy using the shared state $\ket{\varphi_1}$ will succeed in the Pauli braiding test with probability $1$ (since it is honest and $\wg=1$), and in the energy test with probability at least  $\omega_{\energy} - O(\eps^{d})$.  
1759		Since this strategy implements valid logical $X$ and $Z$ operators in the energy test, by
1760	  lemma~\ref{lem:energy_test} it passes   the  test
1761	  with probability at most $\omega^*_{\energy}(H)$. Thus
1762	 $   \omega_{\energy} \leq \omega^*_{\energy}(H) + O({\eps^{d}}) $, and
1763	\begin{align*}
1764	    \omega_{\rm cheat} &= (1-p)(1 - \eps) + p\, \omega_{\energy} \\
1765	                   &\leq (1-p)(1- \eps) + p\, \omega_{\energy}^*(H) + O(p\, \eps^{d}) \\
1766	    &\leq \omega_{\rm honest}(H) - (1-p)\eps + O(p\,{\eps^{d}}).
1767	  \end{align*}
1768	  Choosing $p$ to be a sufficiently small constant times $\eta^{1-d}$, 
1769	  for all $0\leq \eps\leq 1$ this  expression is
1770	  less than or equal to $\omega_{\rm honest}(H) + \eta$. 
1771	\end{proof}
