1906	\subsection{Amplification}
1907	\label{sec:amplification}
1908	
1909	In this section we show how Theorem~\ref{thm:main} can be used to
1910	obtain Corollary~\ref{cor:qma}. The main idea consists in leveraging the fact that our protocol does not require locality of the Hamiltonian to first ``brute-force'' amplify the
1911	gap of the underlying instance of the local Hamiltonian problem to a constant, and then run the protocol on the amplified non-local instance. This is achieved
1912	 by first shifting the Hamiltonian by the appropriate multiple of
1913	identity so that the energy in the yes-instance is less than or equal
1914	to $0$. The gap is amplified by taking sufficiently many tensor product copies of the Hamiltonian, resulting in a nonlocal instance.
1915	
1916	\begin{lemma}[Gap amplification]
1917	Let $H$ be an $n$-qubit Hamiltonian with
1918	minimum energy $\lambda_{\min}(H)\geq 0$ and such that $\|H\|\leq 1$. 
1919	  Let $p(n), q(n)$ be polynomials such that $p(n) >
1920	  q(n)$ for all $n$. Let 
1921		$$H' =  \Id^{\otimes a} - (\Id - ( H - a^{-1}\Id))^{\otimes a},\qquad \text{where}\qquad a = \Big(\frac{1}{q}-\frac{1}{p}\Big)^{-1}.$$
1922		Then $H'$ is a (non-local) Hamiltonian over $an = O(np(n))$
1923	        qubits with $\|H'\|=O(1)$, such that if $\lambda_{\min}(H) \leq 1/p$, then $\lambda_{min}(H') \leq 1/2$, whereas if $\lambda_{\min}(H) \geq 1/q$, then $\lambda_{min}(H') \geq 1$.
1924	  \label{lem:amplify}
1925	\end{lemma}
1926	
1927	\begin{proof}
1928	The proof follows by observing that $\lambda_{\min}(H') = 1 - (1 - (\lambda_{min}(H) - a^{-1}))^a$, and $(1 \pm \delta)^k = 1 \pm k\delta +
1929	  O(\delta^2)$ when $k\delta = O(1)$. 
1930	\end{proof}
1931	
1932	\begin{proof}[Proof of Corollary~\ref{cor:qma}]
1933	By applying the result of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} to the Hamiltonian $H'$ obtained from $H$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:amplify}, we obtain the statement of Corollary~\ref{cor:qma}, except with $p_{\rm c} = p$ and $p_{\rm s} = q $ for some constants $0 < q < p < 1$. To match the constants in the statement of Corollary~\ref{cor:qma}, we make the verifier {automatically} accept with probability $1-p'$, and perform the test with probability $p'$, for some $0\leq p'\leq 1$. Then we get $p_{\rm c} = 1-p' +p'p $ and $p_{\rm s} = 1 - p' + p'q$. If $p'$ is chosen as $p' = \frac{1}{2(1 + p -2q)}$, we get $p_{\rm c} = 1/2 + 2\eta_0$ and $p_{\rm s} = 1/2 + \eta_0$ as desired, with $\eta_0 = \frac{(p-q)}{2(1 + p - 2q)}$.
1934	\end{proof}
1935	
1936	\section{Delegated Computation}
1937	\label{sec:delegated}
1938	It was noticed in~\cite{FitzsimonsH15} that an interactive proof system for the local Hamiltonian problem can also be used for delegated quantum computation with so-called \emph{post-hoc} verification. The key idea is to use the Feynman-Kitaev construction to produce a Hamiltonian encoding the desired computation; measuring the ground energy of this Hamiltonian reveals whether the computation accepts or rejects. 
1939	%This connection was used in~\cite{FitzsimonsH15} to give a delegated computation scheme with a limited quantum verifier, with only the capability to send, receive, and measure a constant number of qubits. 
1940	Following the same connection, we are able to give a post-hoc verifiable delegated computation scheme with a purely classical verifier and a constant number of players. The players only need the power of BQP. The scheme has a constant completeness-soundness gap independent of the size of the circuit to be computed, unlike the scheme of~\cite{FitzsimonsH15} and the classical scheme of~\cite{ReichardtUV13nature}, which both have inverse-polynomial gaps. However, unlike the scheme of~\cite{ReichardtUV13nature} (and similarly to the one in~\cite{FitzsimonsH15}), our protocol is not \emph{blind}: the verifier must reveal the entire circuit to be computed to all the players before the verification process starts.
1941	
1942	\begin{theorem}
1943	There exists an interactive proof system for BQP with seven quantum entangled players and one classical verifier, with one round of communication, in which the player sends $O(\poly(n))$-bit questions and receives $O(1)$-bit answers. The honest players only need the power of BQP.
1944	\end{theorem}
1945	
1946	\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
1947	  For any poly-size quantum circuit $C$, we construct the history
1948	  Hamiltonian $H_C$ and announce to the seven players. In the honest
1949	  case, the players produce the state
1950	  \[ \ket{\psi}  = \mathrm{ENC}\Big(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}} \sum_{t = 1}^{T}
1951	  \ket{t}_{\text{clock}} \ot \ket{\psi_t}\Big), \]
1952	  where $\mathrm{ENC}$ is the encoding map of the 7-qubit code, $t$ labels the clock states of the computation from $1$ to
1953	  $T$, and $\ket{\psi_t}$ is the state of the circuit $C$ at step $t$. This
1954	  state can be prepared with a BQP machine. The players are then
1955	  separated; in the honest case, each player receives a share of the
1956	  encoded state $\ket{\psi}$. The verifier plays the game
1957	  of~\thmref{main} with the players and accepts if and only if they succeed.
1958	\end{proof}
